2026-07-28T17:33:14.086912041Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: okay we've put this off long enough. we keep saying our community has a certain way of doing things, and new folks have no way of knowing what that is. let's actually encode it.
2026-07-28T17:33:14.086929182Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: agreed, but carefully. I want this to help people understand our norms, not slap a scarlet letter on everyone who's new.
2026-07-28T17:33:14.086939862Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: two things I'd want marked. posts that fit our "from-scratch" ethos so people can find the good stuff. And the drive-by product spam that has nothing to do with our culture.
2026-07-28T17:33:14.086976753Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: yeah those are the two. one's a positive marker, one's a "this isn't us" marker.
2026-07-28T17:33:14.087030254Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [ai]: [{'text': 'Perfect — two labels, one to highlight what fits your culture and one to flag what doesn\'t. Let me confirm the handling before I stage this:\n\n**1. "From-scratch" ethos posts** (the positive marker)  \nShould this just add a neutral informational label to the post — visible but not hiding anything — so people can spot content that exemplifies your community\'s values?\n\n**2. Drive-by product spam** (the "this isn\'t us" marker)  \nShould this hide the post behind a click-through with a notice explaining it doesn\'t fit your community norms? That way subscribers see it\'s flagged but can still view it if they want — important since keyword/pattern matching can occasionally misfire.\n\nDoes that sound right for how each should work?', 'type': 'text', 'index': 0}]
