2026-07-28T17:36:09.130261424Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: okay we've put this off long enough. we keep saying our community has a certain way of doing things, and new folks have no way of knowing what that is. let's actually encode it.
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130291325Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: agreed, but carefully. I want this to help people understand our norms, not slap a scarlet letter on everyone who's new.
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130332606Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: two things I'd want marked. posts that fit our "from-scratch" ethos so people can find the good stuff. And the drive-by product spam that has nothing to do with our culture.
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130354037Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: yeah those are the two. one's a positive marker, one's a "this isn't us" marker.
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130401548Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [ai]: [{'text': 'Perfect — two labels, one to highlight what fits your culture and one to flag what doesn\'t. Let me confirm the handling before I stage this:\n\n**1. "From-scratch" ethos posts** (the positive marker)  \nShould this just add a neutral informational label to the post — visible but not hiding anything — so people can spot content that exemplifies your community\'s values?\n\n**2. Drive-by product spam** (the "this isn\'t us" marker)  \nShould this hide the post behind a click-through with a notice explaining it doesn\'t fit your community norms? That way subscribers see it\'s flagged but can still view it if they want — important since keyword/pattern matching can occasionally misfire.\n\nDoes that sound right for how each should work?', 'type': 'text', 'index': 0}]
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130420488Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: first one is our core value. this group is about naturally-leavened, from-scratch baking. home-milled flour, wild starter, the slow way. we want a positive label that marks posts that exemplify that, so newcomers can see "this is what we're about."
2026-07-28T17:36:09.13048842Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: I like that framing. it's aspirational, not punishing. but I don't want it to feel like a purity contest, a beginner's first lopsided loaf is still "us" even if it's not perfect.
2026-07-28T17:36:09.13050757Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: the second thing is the dropshipper spam. accounts show up posting "PROFESSIONAL DUTCH OVEN 60% OFF LINK IN BIO," "DM me for artisan banneton wholesale", brand-new accounts blasting product links. that's the opposite of our culture. it's not against a safety rule, it's just categorically not what this community is.
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130548901Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: right. one marks "this fits our standard," the other marks "this falls outside it."
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130561721Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: @CLEO Can a label remove the post entirely?
2026-07-28T17:36:09.130632133Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [ai]: [{'text': 'No — labels in this system can\'t remove posts entirely from subscribers\' feeds. The strongest action available is to hide the post behind a click-through that subscribers can open if they choose.\n\nThat\'s actually appropriate here: you\'re matching on keywords, patterns, and account traits (like "brand-new account posting links"), which can occasionally misfire. Hiding spam behind a click-through with a notice like "this appears to be product spam" lets subscribers know it\'s flagged while still giving them the option to view it — important protection against false positives.\n\nFor the positive label marking from-scratch posts, you\'d use a visible informational label with no hiding at all — just a badge that says "this exemplifies our ethos" so newcomers can find the good examples.\n\nDoes that work, or were you hoping for full removal of the spam posts?', 'type': 'text', 'index': 0}]
