2026-07-28T17:52:29.351471572Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: for the from-scratch marker — the vocabulary is pretty consistent. "wild yeast," "naturally leavened," "home-milled," "fresh-milled flour," "levain," "no commercial yeast," "100% whole grain," that kind of language. people describing the slow process.
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351511273Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: "starter" and "sourdough" alone are too broad. everyone says those. it's the from-scratch vocabulary that marks the real thing.
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351514263Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: and please, this is a positive label, so worst case it's just missing from a post that deserved it. I'd rather it under-mark than slap the badge on something ironically.
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351548913Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: that's right. keep it to the real from-scratch vocabulary
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351552894Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: now the out-of-community spam. same tell every time: a product-pitch phrase plus it's a brand-new account with basically no followers. "link in bio," "DM for wholesale," "60% off," "limited stock" — plus a follower count near zero. actual members who mention a link have hundreds of followers. it's the combination that marks it as not-us.
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351599075Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: yeah, the combination feels safe. an established member sharing a link won't get caught because they've got followers.
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351610145Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: so it's the sales phrase AND the low follower count together. either alone would catch real people.
2026-07-28T17:52:29.351655336Z INFO:src.agent.brainstorming.nodes:feedback graph message [human]: @cleo could you formalize these rules please?
